g [Estimate].\n  * Economic cycle: furniture demand explicitly \"soft\", \"pressured by weak confidence, affordability\n    constraints, and macroeconomic uncertainty related to the Middle East conflict\" [Actual \u2014 CEO remark,\n    8/17/2026]. Consumer-cyclical late-stage / early-slowing [Estimate].\n  * Inflation: \"elevated inflation, rising energy costs, rising inflationary pressures\" [Actual \u2014 company].\n  * Credit spreads: [Unconfirmed]. Investment-grade spreads ~+90\u2013110bp [Assumption].\n\nStep 1-2. Regime probability distribution (sums to 100%):\n  * Reflation (high rates + growth): 20%  \u2014 Fed high-ish but economy muddling through\n  * Stagflation (high rates + low growth): 40% \u2014 current conditions: weak consumer + rising energy + 10Y 4.7%\n  * Recession (low rates + low growth): 25%   \u2014 2027 downturn scenario\n  * Goldilocks (low rates + growth): 15%      \u2014 requires Fed cuts + consumer revival\n  Rationale: the conjoint evidence \u2014 10Y at 4.7, Fed at 3.50\u20133.75, weak consumer confidence, elevated\n  energy/inflation \u2014 clusters around a stagflation-lite regime. [Estimate]\n\nStep 1-3. Macro sensitivity (each \u22123 to +3):\n  * Interest-rate sensitivity: \u22122 (housing/credit-sensitive; furniture financing)\n  * Dollar sensitivity: \u22121 (USD strength helps import costs but hurts peso translation; mixed)\n  * Liquidity sensitivity: \u22121 (discretionary durables)\n  * Economic-cycle sensitivity: \u22123 (core cyclical)\n  * Commodity/energy sensitivity: \u22122 (foam, polyols, lumber, freight)\n\nStep 1-4. Macro alignment: EV of macro impact = 0.20\u00d7(\u22121) + 0.40\u00d7(\u22123) + 0.25\u00d7(\u22123) + 0.15\u00d7(+2)\n  = \u22120.2 \u22121.2 \u22120.75 +0.3 = \u22121.85 \u2192 HEADWIND.\n  \u2192 Conviction \u22120.5 step. No change to intrinsic value; strengthens split-entry discipline.\n\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500