flexivity would flip if cash/availability eroded or tariffs spiked \u2192 monitor liquidity. [Estimate]\n\nStep 3.5-3. Customer behavior: value-consciousness rising even at higher price points [Actual \u2014 CEO];\n  RTA (homestyles) exit signals preference for durable soft seating; health/wellness is the adoption wedge\n  into younger cohorts. LTV/CAC: not disclosed [Unconfirmed].\n\nSECTION 4. SCENARIO ENGINE (BAYESIAN)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nPriors (1.5): cyclical-peak drawdown base rate 30\u201340%; margin persistence 25\u201335%.\nLikelihood evidence: 11 straight quarters of growth; FY26 adjusted margin 7.5%; record FCF; 24% buyback;\n  BUT Q4 adjusted OI fell YoY ($8.2M vs $10.3M); backlog \u221211.8% QoQ; Q1 FY27 guide 6.5\u20137.0% (below Q4's 7.1%).\nPosteriors (Probability must sum to 100%; base case highest):\n  \u2022 BEAR 30% \u2014 IV $50 (\u221238%): US furniture recession 2027; revenue \u221215% over two years, OM reverts to 4%,\n    accumulated WC release fails to repeat; price tests $50.\n  \u2022 BASE 50% \u2014 IV $95 (+17%): guided FY27 achieved; margins grind to 8.5% by FY31; rates drift down after\n    2027; buyback completes as planned; net debt ~$60M.\n  \u2022 BULL 20% \u2014 IV $130 (+61%): demand stabilizes, tariffs roll back, OM 9.5%+ by FY29, additional buybacks.\nExpected price = 0.30\u00d750 + 0.50\u00d795 + 0.20\u00d7130 = 15 + 47.5 + 26 = $88.5 [Estimate].\n  Base-case MoS = (95 \u2212 80.93)/95 = 14.8% \u2192 limited-to-adequate.\n  Expected-value MoS = (88.5 \u2212 80.93)/88.5 = 8.6% \u2192 LIMITED.\n  50% CI: $75\u2013110. 80% CI: $50\u2013130. [Estimate]\n\nSECTION 4.5. TIME-HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nMarket-implied horizon: at $80.93, market is paying 16.4x FY26 adjusted EPS ($4.94) [Arithmetic], and\n  roughly 13.7x FY27 normalized EPS (~$5.9) \u2014 i.e., ~1\u20132 years of forward earnings, with sell-side\n  12-month targets ($69\u201372) below spot [Aggregator, stale 7/22].\nMy horizon: 3\u20135 years. Spread = ~2 years \u2192 mild positive arbitrage exists, but thin coverage (1\u20132\n  analysts) means price discovery is weak; the \"arbitrage\" is real but small. [Estimate]\n\nSECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nScore (total /15):\n  1. Business quality: 3/3 (narrow widening moat, 11 growth quarters, FCF machine)\n  2. Valuation attractiveness: 2/3 (Base IV ~17% above; expected value only ~9% above)\n  3. Macro alignment: 1/3 (stagflation-leaning; furniture demand soft; rates 4.7%)\n  4. Thesis clarity: 3/3 (KPIs: guided margin 6.5\u20137%, backlog, buyback count, FCF)\n  5. Execution traceability: 3/3 (record adjusted EPS $4.94; FCF $47.5M; buyback completed;