lobal liquidity: moderately restrictive; Fed in a hold pattern, balance-sheet run-off ongoing [Estimate].\n  * Economic cycle: furniture demand explicitly \"soft\", \"pressured by weak confidence, affordability\n    constraints, and macroeconomic uncertainty related to the Middle East conflict\" [Actual \u2014 CEO remark,\n    8/17/2026]. Consumer-cyclical late-stage / early-slowing [Estimate].\n  * Inflation: \"elevated inflation, rising energy costs, rising inflationary pressures\" [Actual \u2014 company].\n  * Credit spreads: [Unconfirmed]. Investment-grade spreads ~+90\u2013110bp [Assumption].\n\nStep 1-2. Regime probability distribution (sums to 100%):\n  * Reflation (high rates + growth): 20%  \u2014 Fed high-ish but economy muddling through\n  * Stagflation (high rates + low growth): 40% \u2014 current conditions: weak consumer + rising energy + 10Y 4.7%\n  * Recession (low rates + low growth): 25%   \u2014 2027 downturn scenario\n  * Goldilocks (low rates + growth): 15%      \u2014 requires Fed cuts + consumer revival\n  Rationale: the conjoint evidence \u2014 10Y at 4.7, Fed at 3.50\u20133.75, weak consumer confidence, elevated\n  energy/inflation \u2014 clusters around a stagflation-lite regime. [Estimate]\n\nStep 1-3. Macro sensitivity (each \u22123 to +3):\n  * Interest-rate sensitivity: \u22122 (housing/credit-sensitive; furniture financing)\n  * Dollar sensitivity: \u22121 (USD strength helps import costs but hurts peso translation; mixed)\n  * Liquidity sensitivity: \u22121 (discretionary durables)\n  * Economic-cycle sensitivity: \u22123 (core cyclical)\n  * Commodity/energy sensitivity: \u22122 (foam, polyols, lumber, freight)\n\nStep 1-4. Macro alignment: EV of macro impact = 0.20\u00d7(\u22121) + 0.40\u00d7(\u22123) + 0.25\u00d7(\u22123) + 0.15\u00d7(+2)\n  = \u22120.2 \u22121.2 \u22120.75 +0.3 = \u22121.85 \u2192 HEADWIND.\n  \u2192 Conviction \u22120.5 step. No change to intrinsic value; strengthens split-entry discipline.\n\nSECTION 1.5. BASE RATE & HISTORICAL PATTERN\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 1.5-1. Base rates:\n  * Cyclical peak \u2192 40%+ stock drop within 3 years: 30\u201340% base rate. FLXS has just run +86% in six months\n    (price 32.65\u201377.19 52-wk range, now 80.93) [Actual, market data] \u2014 the bear scenario probability is\n    set at 30%, at the low end of the base rate, justified below.\n  * 5-year enterprise survival: very high (>90%) for a 130-year-old, FCF-positive incumbent [Estimate].\n  * Sustained profitable operation 5 years after losses (FY2019\u201320): ~70\u201375% [Estimate].\n  * Sustaining an 8.5%+ operating margin through a full cycle (i.e., bull case): 25\u201335% base rate [Estimate].\n\nStep 1.5-2. Deviation of thesis from base rate:\n  The base case assumes OP margin averages ~7.5% over 10 years vs 5.5% 12-year median. Justification for\n  above-base persistence (requires \u22653 specific reasons):\n    1. Mix shift: exiting low-margin homestyles RTA category (a \u221270bp Q4 drag being removed) while\n       health/wellness and strategic accounts grow [Actual \u2014 company].\n    2. Near-shoring in Mexico: cost advantage and tariff/FX mitigation vs offshore peers; 100bp peso FX\n       drag in Q4 was the offset, not structural margin erosion [Actual + Estimate].\n    3. Capital discipline: 24% share count reduction at $47, dividend raised 25% \u2014 equity base is leaner,\n       so per-share FCF holds even if dollar margins revert partially [Actual].\n  Reasonable but not overwhelming \u2192 base-rate drag kept: margins fade toward 8.5%, not 10%+.\n\nStep 1.5-3. Base-rate shock check: bull probability 20% vs ~30% base rate for peak-margin persistence \u2192\n  below 1.0x, no red flag. Success probability (base+bull = 70%) vs survival base rate ~75% \u2192 no flag.\n\nSECTION 2. BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2-1. Business in 3 sentences: FLXS designs, manufactures, imports, and markets residential\n  upholstered furniture (sofas, recliners, motion furniture) in the US, selling through retail partners\n  and online. It competes on durability/premium construction (patented Blue Steel Spring) and is\n  repositioning into health/wellness and strategic-account channels. It operates a Mexico near-shore\n  manufacturing footprint with a US sales/distribution base.\n  Understandability: HIGH.\n\nStep 2-2. Moat (0\u20133 each):\n  Intangible assets (brand 130yrs, patent, wellness category) 2; Switching costs (retail partner\n  relationships) 1; Network effects 0; Cost advantage (Mexico near-shore) 2; Economies of scale 1;\n  Regulatory barriers 0; Data moat (consumer insights) 1 \u2192 TOTAL 7 = NARROW moat.\n  Direction: WIDENING \u2014 11 consecutive quarters of market-share gains, RTA exit improves pricing mix,\n  category expansion. [Actual trends + Estimate]\n\nStep 2-3. Lynch classification: CYCLICAL (recovering). Checkpoints: revenue trajectory tied to housing/\n  consumer cycle (FY21 $479M \u2192 FY23 $394M \u2192 FY26 $459M); margins variable (FY19 \u22129.7% to FY26 +9.3%);\n  balance sheet now the swing factor for per-share value (buybacks). Also shows TURNAROUND characteristics\n  (post-FY2019/20 losses). Classification: Cyclical-first.\n\nStep 2-4. Financial strength scorecard:\n  \u2022 Revenue growth, 3-yr (FY23\u2192FY26): +16.5% cumulative, ~5.2% CAGR [Arithmetic]\n  \u2022 Operating margin: 3-yr avg 6.4%; latest 9.3% GAAP / 7.5% adjusted [Arithmetic + Actual]\n  \u2022 EPS growth: GAAP diluted FY25 $3.55 \u2192 FY26 $6.07; adjusted $4.17 \u2192 $4.94 [Actual \u2014 company]\n  \u2022 ROE: 18.1% [Aggregator]; ROIC (GAAP): ~15% [Estimate]\n  \u2022 FCF margin FY26: 47.5/459.2 = 10.3%; FCF conversion vs net income: 144% (strong, accruals \u22120.0686) [Arithmetic]\n  \u2022 Net debt/EBITDA: ~1.0\u20131.3x incl. leases (see 2.5); interest coverage > 20x [Estimate]\n  \u2022 Structure: post-buyback revolver debt + operating leases; cash $16.7M + $54.1M undrawn revolver\n    availability [Actual \u2014 company press release]\n  Judgment: GOOD (excellent FCF/capital returns; ordinary margins and cyclical exposure).\n\nStep 2-5. Capital allocation scorecard (0\u20133 each, total /15):\n  1. Buyback efficiency: 3 \u2014 1.28M shares at $47 (~24% of shares) transacted at a 2.5% discount, ~41%\n     below current price; year-long buyback program completed $64M in FY26 [Actual]\n  2. M&A track record: 1 \u2014 no value-accretive M&A; strategic exit of homestyles RTA (a portfolio delete) [Actual]\n  3. Capex efficiency: 2 \u2014 $3.9M capex on $47.5M FCF; incremental ROIC high but reinvestment is minimal [Actual + Estimate]\n  4. Rationality of mix: 3 \u2014 buyback + dividend +25% + organic innovation funding, explicitly stated [Actual]\n  5. Guidance accuracy: 2 \u2014 Q1/Q2 FY26 beat; guidance intermittently paused (tariff uncertainty) [Actual]\n  Insider bonus: management/family +14.67% [Aggregator] \u2192 +1. Bertsch family sell-down was a\n  liquidity/diversification event at a discount approved by a special committee [Actual] \u2192 \u22121.\n  TOTAL: 11/15 \u2014 GOOD.\n\nSECTION 2.5. INTANGIBLES-ADJUSTED FINANCIALS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 2.5-1. R&D capitalization: M&A-level R&D not separately disclosed; furniture R&D immaterial and\n  expensed within SG&A. No adjustment. [Assumption \u2014 no disclosed R&D line in pack]\n\nStep 2.5-2. SBC: FY26 SBC $5.0M [Actual \u2014 pack]. Adjusted FCF = FCF 47.5 \u2212 SBC 5.0 = $42.5M [Arithmetic].\n  I model from GAAP operating income, so SBC is already expensed in my DCF (NOPAT basis) \u2014 no double count.\n\nStep 2.5-3. Lease capitalization: operating leases $52.0M at 6/30/2026 [Actual \u2014 pack 6B]. Treating lease\n  obligations as debt, total debt \u2248 $52M leases + drawn revolver. Net debt range: $35M (low) / $60M\n  (central) / $85M (high). Central picks: $52M leases + ~$25M drawn revolver \u2212 $16.7M cash = ~$60M\n  [Estimate]. Note: vendor \"total debt $52M\" appears to equal the lease line only; the drawn revolver is\n  not separately held \u2192 the discrepancy is declared, not resolved. Sensitivity shown in Section 3.\n\nStep 2.5-4. Brand/data assets: no quantified brand valuation retrieved \u2192 not added [Unconfirmed].\n\nStep 2.5-5. Adjusted ROIC: Adjusted NOPAT = adjusted OI $34.4M \u00d7 (1 \u2212 23% tax) = $26.5M [Arithmetic].\n  Adjusted invested capital \u2248 equity $133M + debt $60M \u2212 cash $17M = $176M [Estimate].\n  Adjusted ROIC = 26.5/176 = 15.1% [Estimate] vs GAAP ROE 18.1% [Aggregator]. Gap ~3pp \u2014 explained by\n  lease liabilities and the 2026 equity dip after the buyback. No red flag.\n\nStep 2.5-6. Owner earnings (Buffett-style): NI $33M + D&A ~$10M (see flag) \u2212 maintenance capex ~$4M \u2212\n  \u0394WC $3M normalized = ~$36M [Estimate]. D&A is NOT in the pack for any year [Unconfirmed] \u2192 D&A of\n  $10M is an [Assumption] based on net PP&E of $36M and historical norms.\n\nSECTION 3. VALUATION (Engine 2 \u00d7 60% + Engine 1 \u00d7 40%)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 3-1. Forecast path \u2014 ALL DRIVERS SOURCED:\n\nDiscount-rate derivation (WACC):\n  Rf 4.7% [Actual \u2014 FRED 10Y]; ERP 5.0% [Assumption \u2014 long-run US equity premium]; Beta: vendor 0.581\n  is [Aggregator, window unstated and, for a high-beta cyclical, implausibly low \u2014 REJECTED as raw input].\n  Adjusted beta 1.1 [Assumption \u2014 cyclicality, small-cap, illiquidity]. Cost of equity = 4.7 + 1.1\u00d75.0 =\n  10.2% [Estimate]. Cost of debt ~5.5% pre-tax [Assumption], w_d ~10% \u2192 WACC \u2248 10.0% [Estimate].\n  Terminal growth 2.5% [Assumption \u2014 \u2264 long-run nominal GDP, consistent with 10Y 4.7% and a mature category].\n\nRevenue growth sources:\n  \u2022 FY2027: guided +1% to +4% (Q1 guide $111\u2013115M) [Actual \u2014 company 8/17/2026]. Model: +2.5% \u2192 $471M.\n  \u2022 FY2028\u201329: pack consensus +3.9% revenue [Aggregator \u2014 yfinance, 34d stale] \u2192 use +3%/+3% [Estimate].\n  \u2022 FY2030\u201331: fade to +2% [Assumption \u2014 furniture demand long-run GDP-ish].\n  \u2022 FY2032\u201336: +2% then terminal 2.5% \u2014 NOTE: after FY27, NO company guidance exists; the path is an\n    [Assumption]. Scenario section shows the alternative (revenue \u22125% for two years).\n\nOperating margin sources:\n  \u2022 FY2027: guidance 6.5%\u20137.0% Q1; full-year model 7.0% [Actual Q1 guide + Assumption for remaining quarters].\n  \u2022 FY2028: 7.5% [Assumption \u2014 mix shift, homestyles exit, near-shore benefit].\n  \u2022 FY2029: 8.0%; FY2030: 8.3%; FY2031 onward: 8.5% ceiling [Assumption \u2014 deliberately capped below both\n    GAAP FY26 9.3% and peer peaks, reflecting 5.5% 12-yr median gravity]. NOTE: no guidance beyond FY27;\n    margin path is the primary valuation variable \u2014 tested in the Tornado.\n\nCapex: FY26 actual $3.9M [Actual]; model $4.5\u20136.5M [Assumption \u2014 maintenance-heavy, low-growth].\nD&A: $10M base rising with capex [Assumption \u2014 not in pack, flagged [Unconfirmed]].\n\u0394WC: FY26 actual swing \u2212$28M (working capital SOAKED $28M: receivables/inventory build) [Arithmetic].\n  Normalize to \u2212$3M/year consumption [Assumption \u2014 reversal risk noted in Red Team].\nTax rate: 23% = Q4 FY26 actual effective rate [Actual \u2014 company].\n\nFORECAST TABLE (USD M; fiscal year ends ~June 30):\n              FY27   FY28   FY29   FY30   FY31   FY32   FY33   FY34   FY35   FY36\nRevenue       471.0  485.1  499.7  514.7  530.1  546.0  562.4  579.3  596.7  614.6\nOp margin      7.0%   7.5%   8.0%   8.3%   8.5%   8.5%   8.5%   8.5%   8.5%   8.5%\nOperating inc  33.0   36.4   40.0   42.7   45.1   46.4   47.8   49.2   50.7   52.2\nNOPAT (23%)    25.4   28.0   30.8   32.9   34.7   35.7   36.8   37.9   39.0   40.2\n+D&A           10.0   10.5   11.0   11.5   12.0   12.5   13.0   13.5   14.0   14.5\n\u2212Capex          4.5    5.0    5.0    5.5    5.5    6.0    6.0    6.0    6.5    6.5\n\u2212\u0394WC            3.0    3.0    3.0    3.0    3.0    3.0    3.0    3.0    3.0    3.0\nFCFF           27.9   30.5   33.8   35.9   38.2   39.2   40.8   42.4   43.5   45.2\n\nPV @10%: 25.4+25.2+25.4+24.5+23.7+22.1+20.9+19.8+18.4+17.4 = 222.8\nTerminal value = 45.2\u00d71.025/(0.10\u22120.025) = $617.3; PV = 617.3\u00d70.3855 = 238.0\nEnterprise value = 460.8\nLess central net debt 60.0 \u2192 equity 400.8 \u2192 /4.076M shares = $98.4 \u2248 $98 [Estimate]\n(DCF sensitivity: net debt 35 \u2192 $104; net debt 85 \u2192 $92)\n\nStep 3-2. Multiples / normalized cross-check (Engine 2):\n  Normalized mid-cycle: revenue $475M \u00d7 normalized OM 7.0% = $33.3M OI \u2192 NOPAT 25.6 \u2192 normalized FCFF\n  \u2248 $27M \u2192 FCFF/share $6.6; fair normalized multiple 12\u201314x \u2192 $79\u201393; mid $88 [Estimate].\n  Blend: 0.6\u00d798.4 + 0.4\u00d788 = $94.2. Central Base IV = $95 [Estimate].\n  (Vendor trailing P/E 13.7x, P/B 2.16x, PEG 1.24 [Aggregator, stale 7/22] are consistent with \"fair,\n   not cheap\" at current price.)\n\nStep 3-X. Tornado sensitivity (\u00b11\u03c3) \u2014 Top 3 Thesis KPIs:\n  1. FY27\u201329 operating margin (\u00b11.0 pt): IV $77 \u2194 $113\n  2. WACC (\u00b11.0 pt): IV $80 \u2194 $112\n  3. Terminal growth (\u00b10.75 pt): IV $86 \u2194 $103\n  \u2192 Margins and rates dominate. These become Section 8 monitoring KPIs.\n\nSECTION 3.5. SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nStep 3.5-1. Competitive response: If FLXS sustains share gains in a soft market, Ashley/La-Z-Boy/Costco\n  suppliers can respond with price. Impact: \u22121 (moderate) \u2014 FLXS's premium/durability positioning and\n  near-shore cost base give some insulation, but price wars in furniture are historically common. [Estimate]\n\nStep 3.5-2. Reflexivity (Soros): HIGH currently. +86% stock + 24% share shrink + record FCF \u2192 SBC\n  attractiveness for talent, buyback currency optionality, retailer confidence (perceived stability).\n  Negative reflexivity would flip if cash/availability eroded or tariffs spiked \u2192 monitor liquidity. [Estimate]\n\nStep 3.5-3. Customer behavior: value-consciousness rising even at higher price points [Actual \u2014 CEO];\n  RTA (homestyles) exit signals preference for durable soft seating; health/wellness is the adoption wedge\n  into younger cohorts. LTV/CAC: not disclosed [Unconfirmed].\n\nSECTION 4. SCENARIO ENGINE (BAYESIAN)\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nPriors (1.5): cyclical-peak drawdown base rate 30\u201340%; margin persistence 25\u201335%.\nLikelihood evidence: 11 straight quarters of growth; FY26 adjusted margin 7.5%; record FCF; 24% buyback;\n  BUT Q4 adjusted OI fell YoY ($8.2M vs $10.3M); backlog \u221211.8% QoQ; Q1 FY27 guide 6.5\u20137.0% (below Q4's 7.1%).\nPosteriors (Probability must sum to 100%; base case highest):\n  \u2022 BEAR 30% \u2014 IV $50 (\u221238%): US furniture recession 2027; revenue \u221215% over two years, OM reverts to 4%,\n    accumulated WC release fails to repeat; price tests $50.\n  \u2022 BASE 50% \u2014 IV $95 (+17%): guided FY27 achieved; margins grind to 8.5% by FY31; rates drift down after\n    2027; buyback completes as planned; net debt ~$60M.\n  \u2022 BULL 20% \u2014 IV $130 (+61%): demand stabilizes, tariffs roll back, OM 9.5%+ by FY29, additional buybacks.\nExpected price = 0.30\u00d750 + 0.50\u00d795 + 0.20\u00d7130 = 15 + 47.5 + 26 = $88.5 [Estimate].\n  Base-case MoS = (95 \u2212 80.93)/95 = 14.8% \u2192 limited-to-adequate.\n  Expected-value MoS = (88.5 \u2212 80.93)/88.5 = 8.6% \u2192 LIMITED.\n  50% CI: $75\u2013110. 80% CI: $50\u2013130. [Estimate]\n\nSECTION 4.5. TIME-HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nMarket-implied horizon: at $80.93, market is paying 16.4x FY26 adjusted EPS ($4.94) [Arithmetic], and\n  roughly 13.7x FY27 normalized EPS (~$5.9) \u2014 i.e., ~1\u20132 years of forward earnings, with sell-side\n  12-month targets ($69\u201372) below spot [Aggregator, stale 7/22].\nMy horizon: 3\u20135 years. Spread = ~2 years \u2192 mild positive arbitrage exists, but thin coverage (1\u20132\n  analysts) means price discovery is weak; the \"arbitrage\" is real but small. [Estimate]\n\nSECTION 5. CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\nScore (total /15):\n  1. Business quality: 3/3 (narrow widening moat, 11 growth quarters, FCF machine)\n  2. Valuation attractiveness: 2/3 (Base IV ~17% above; expected value only ~9% above)\n  3. Macro alignment: 1/3 (stagflation-leaning; furniture demand soft; rates 4.7%)\n  4. Thesis clarity: 3/3 (KPIs: guided margin 6.5\u20137%, backlog, buyback count, FCF)\n  5. Execution traceability: 3/3 (record adjusted EPS $4.94; FCF $47.5M; buyback completed; dividend +25%)\n  Total = 12/15 \u2192 HIGH.\n  Adjustments: macro headwind \u22120.5; archetype transition \u22120.5; Red-Team combat gap \u22121.0 (see \u00a79).\n  Effective conviction \u2248 10/15 \u2192 HIGH but capped.\nSizing guidance: target 6.0% max; initial 3.0% (half). Caps apply (cyclical, macro headwind, transition).\n\nSECTION 5.5. BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\n  \u2022 Short interest: 5.07% of float; days-to-cover 3.22; +21.9% MoM [Aggregator, stale 7/22 \u2014 settlement-\n    basis data]. Rising short interest into strength \u2192 squeeze fuel but also informed skepticism.\n  \u2022 Ownership: institutional 65.8%, insider 14.7% (post-Bertsch sale) [Aggregator]. Passive/ETF ownership\n    not separately held [Unconfirmed].\n  \u2022 Momentum: +86% in six months [Estimate \u2014 market data]; price above ALL sell-side targets (69\u201372 stale).\n  \u2022 Sentiment: extended; the 24% tender at $47 created a scarcity float (4.08M shares) \u2192 amplified moves.\n  \u2022 Options positioning: [Unconfirmed \u2014 no data].\n  \u2022 Crowding risk score: 9/15 (scarcity float + momentum + event-driven run + thin coverage).\n    \u2192 position size reduction \u22121.0 pp applied in Section 5/12.\n\nSECTION 6. PORTFOLIO FIT\n\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\