capex $3.9M - \u0394WC from cash\nflow ($28M increase, incl. buyback/timing) \u2248 $7.1M for FY2026. Excluding the abnormal WC build and tender:\nunderlying owner earnings \u2248 $33-38M. I will not over-index on a single year's WC.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 3 \u2014 VALUATION ENGINE\n\nEnterprise Archetype: C \u2014 CYCLICAL (furniture durables) \u2192 Engine 2: Cycle-Adjusted Valuation, cross-checked\nwith Engine 1 DCF. Transition to Quality Compounder: <50% met (revenue growth still negative over 5yr; margin\nvolatility remains high) \u2192 not a blended case.\n\nEngine 2 \u2014 Cycle position: FY2022 peak $544M \u2192 FY2023 trough $394M \u2192 FY2026 recovery $459M. We are mid-\ncycle; Q4 FY2026 revenue +0.7% confirms deceleration.\n\nNormalized earnings:\n- Mid-cycle revenue: $455M [Assumption] (midpoint of trough/peak; near trailing actual).\n- Mid-cycle EBIT margin: 8.5% [Assumption] \u2014 between 12-yr median 5.5% and FY2026 9.3%, allowing for tariff\n  compression in FY2027.\n- Normalized EBIT: $38.7M. Tax 24% [Assumption] \u2192 net income $29.4M; plus modest interest income \u2192 $30M.\n- Normalized EPS on 4.076M shares: $7.36 [Arithmetic].\n- Normal multiple: 12x [Assumption] \u2014 justified P/E at 0% growth and 9% WACC \u2248 10.4x; at 2% terminal\n  growth \u2248 13.1x. 12x balances cyclicality vs improved structure and net cash.\n- Cycle-adjusted IV base: 12 \u00d7 $7.36 = $88.3 [Estimate].\n- 50% CI: $78\u2013$98.80; 80% CI: $55\u2013$126.\n\nDCF cross-check (Engine 1):\nWACC derivation:\n- Risk-free: 4.65% [Actual \u2014 10Y Treasury 8/24/26].\n- ERP: 5.0% [Assumption].\n- Beta: 1.0, not 0.581 [Assumption \u2014 small-cap cyclical with 5% short interest and housing-driven demand;\n  vendor's 0.581 likely reflects a lookback with low-vol regime; I use a forward economic beta of 1.0].\n- Cost of equity: 4.65% + 1.0 \u00d7 5.0% = 9.65%.\n- Cost of debt (leases): pre-tax 5.5%, after-tax 4.18% [Assumption].\n- Weights: E = 330/(330+35) = 90.4%; D = 9.6%.\n- WACC = 0.904 \u00d7 9.65% + 0.096 \u00d7 4.18% = 8.72% + 0.40% = 9.1% \u2248 9.0%.\n\nForecast table (all post-FY2026 items are [Assumption]; FY2026 baselines are [Actual]):\n\n| $M | FY2026A | FY2027 | FY2028 | FY2029 | FY2030 | FY2031 |\n|----|---------|--------|--------|--------|--------|--------|\n| Revenue | 459 | 466 | 480 | 497 | 512 | 523 |\n| Growth | \u2014 | +1.5% | +3.0% | +3.5% | +3.0% | +2.1% |\n| EBIT margin | 9.3% | 8.0% | 8.5% | 9.0% | 9.3% | 9.3% |\n| EBIT | 43.0 | 37.3 | 40.8 | 44.7 | 47.6 | 48.6 |\n| NOPAT (24%) | 32.7 | 28.3 | 31.0 | 34.0 | 36.2 | 36.9 |\n| D&A (1% rev) | ~6 | 4.7 | 4.8 | 5.0 | 5.1 | 5.2 |\n| Capex (1% rev) | 3.9 | 4.7 | 4.8 | 5.0 | 5.1 | 5.2 |\n| \u0394WC (1% of \u0394Rev) | \u2014 | -0.1 | -0.1 | -0.2 | -0.2 | -0.1 |\n| FCF | 48.3A | 28.2 | 31.0 | 33.9 | 36.1 | 36.8 |\n\nSources: FY2027 revenue/margin = my assumption informed by Q4 +0.7% actual and management's tariff-driven\nmargin- dilution guidance (H2 FY2026 [Estimate \u2014 research brief \u00a7Catalysts]); FY2028-31 growth/margin =\nmy assumptions (no company guidance beyond FY2026 \u2014 the pack and calls explicitly note FY2027 guidance is\npaused [Estimate]). I stress the path below.\n\nTerminal growth 2.0% [Assumption] \u2014 in a housing-driven cyclical, I cap terminal growth at nominal GDP\n(3.5%) minus long-run furniture unit decline; 2% is conservative.\n\nPV of FCF at 9%: 25.9 + 26.1 + 26.2 + 25.6 + 23.9 = 127.7.\nTerminal value = 36.8\u00d71.02 / (0.09\u20130.02) = 536.2; PV = 348.5.\nEV = 476.2. Equity = EV - net debt (35) + excess cash (none) = $441.2M. Per share = 108.2.\n\nSensitivity (DCF per-share vs terminal growth and WACC):\n\n| WACC\\Term g | 1.0% | 2.0% | 3.0% |\n|-------------|------|------|------|\n| 8.5% | $101 | $121 | $151 |\n| 9.0% | $92 | $108 | $132 |\n| 10.0% | $76 | $88 | $103 |\n\nThe DCF at base assumptions ($108) is skewed above cycle-adjusted IV ($88.3) because FY2026's EBIT margin of\n9.3% is at a cyclical margin peak for this revenue level. I give Engine 2 (cycle-adjusted) 70% weight, DCF\n30% weight:\n- Blended IV = 0.70 \u00d7 $88.3 + 0.30 \u00d7 $108 = $61.8 + $32.4 = $94.2 [Estimate].\n- I then shade down with a conservative 10% for cyclical overconfidence \u2192 **Final central IV = $88.5.**\n\nReverse DCF (what market implies): at EV $365M, WACC 9%, base FCF $42M, implied terminal growth \u2248 0.3%.\nThe market is pricing FLXS as a zero-growth cash machine \u2014 that is the literal \"boring cyclical\" scenario.\nRequired return implied: EV = FCF/(r\u2013g) \u2192 365 = 42/(r\u20130.02) \u2192 r = 13.5%. Market demands a ~13.5% return on a\nnet-cash, 15.5% ROIC asset. The return hurdle is high but the asset clears it.\n\nTornado (top-3 IV drivers):\n1. Normalized EBIT margin (5.5% \u2192 11%) \u2194 IV $50 \u2192 $125.\n2. Normal multiple (9x \u2192 15x) \u2194 IV $62 \u2192 $104.\n3. Terminal growth (0% \u2192 3%) \u2194 IV $92 \u2192 $132.\nThe top driver = margins, which is exactly where tariffs hit. Top KPIs: gross margin %, operating margin %,\nrevenue growth, order/backlog.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 3.5 \u2014 SECOND-ORDER EFFECTS\n\n3.5-1. Competitive response: If FLXS's premium positioning keeps winning share, Ashley/Rooms To Go and\nimporters will counter with price/private-label pressure. Effect: -1 to -2 (margin ceiling).\n3.5-2. Reflexivity: Positive \u2014 higher stock price enables SBC attraction, and net-cash status reassures\nretail partners. Negative reflexivity historically strong for furniture (FY2023 inventory destock).\n3.5-3. Customer behavior: B2B retail buyers matter more than end-consumer brand. Omni-channel shift is slow.\nLTV/CAC: [Unconfirmed] \u2014 not disclosed.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 4 \u2014 SCENARIOS (BAYESIAN UPDATED)\n\nPrior (from base rates): base 45%, bull 30%, bear 25%.\nLikelihood updates from data:\n- Q4 FY2026 revenue +0.7% [Actual] \u2014 deceleration \u2192 bear +5pp.\n- Record diluted FY2026 EPS [Actual] \u2014 bull +5pp.\n- Tariff margin dilution warning [Estimate] \u2014 bear +5pp, base -5pp.\n- 24% tender at $47 [Actual IR] \u2014 bull +5pp.\n- Activist/strategic interest [Unconfirmed] \u2014 bull +2pp, bear -2pp.\nPosterior:\n- **Bear 25%** \u2014 Recession, tariffs bite, margins fall to 5% net: revenue $420M; NI $21M; EPS $5.15;\n  multiple 8x \u2192 price **$41**.\n- **Base 50%** \u2014 Slow growth, mid-cycle EPS $7.36; multiple 12x \u2192 price **$88.3**.\n- **Bull 25%** \u2014 Housing recovers, tariffs resolved, EPS $9.00; multiple 15x \u2192 price **$135**.\nExpected price = 0.25\u00d741 + 0.50\u00d788.3 + 0.25\u00d7135 = 10.25 + 44.15 + 33.75 = **$88.2** [Estimate].\nMargin of Safety vs T0 $80.93 = 8.2% \u2192 ADEQUATE-to-LIMITED. 80% CI: $41\u2013$135; 50% CI: $70\u2013$110.\n\nBayesian note: the 2022 CSC bid at $20.80 was rejected by the same board that later tendered at $47. A new\ncontrol bid at 12-15x normalized EPS ($88\u2013$110) is plausible if housing stays weak \u2014 this optionality\nsupports the right tail.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 4.5 \u2014 HORIZON ARBITRAGE\n\nMarket horizon: at a 13.5% implied discount rate, the market is pricing essentially zero growth in\nperpetuity \u2014 i.e., no re-rating, no housing-cycle help, no control premium. My scenario horizon: 3\u20135 years\nfor a cyclical recovery to normalize margins. Spread = ~2\u20133 years \u2192 ARBITRAGE OPPORTUNITY EXISTS, but it\nworks against the short-term, not with it. The market is too short-term bearish on housing; if you can hold\n3 years, the expected value skew is mild-positive. If your horizon is <18 months, the market's high hurdle\n(13.5%) is fair.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 5 \u2014 CONVICTION & POSITION SIZING\n\nConviction score (15):\n- Business quality: 3/3\n- Valuation attractiveness: 1/3 (MoS 8.2%)\n- Macro alignment: 1/3\n- Thesis clarity (KPIs measurable): 3/3\n- Execution traceability: 3/3\nTOTAL: **11/15 \u2192 HIGH conviction** (but not Very High \u2014 valuation/Macro caps it).\nConviction decay: apply -1 every 6 months without confirmation \u2192 by Feb 2027, score 10.\n\nGuideline sizing at High conviction: 4\u20136%. Macro headwind \u2192 -1%. Engine 2 cyclical \u2192 -1%. Extreme\noverbought state (short interest +21.9% MoM, price above 52-wk high) \u2192 -1%. **Target: 2\u20133% position.**\nBlend check vs Layer 6.5 Kelly (\u22480\u20132%): gap < 20pp \u2192 acceptable.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 5.5 \u2014 BEHAVIORAL & POSITIONING\n\n- Short interest: 5.07% of float, 182,325 shares, DTC 3.22 d, MoM +21.9% [Actual \u2014 aggregator, stale].\n  Rising short interest into an uptrend = potential squeeze fuel; but also crowded bear overlay.\n- Institutional ownership: 65.76% [Actual \u2014 stale]; insider 14.67%.\n- Price vs 52-wk high: $80.93 > $77.19 \u2014 breakout territory; the 52-week range is stale (through 7/22/26).\n- Analyst targets: mean $70.50, low $69, high $72 [stale 7/22/26] \u2014 all below T0. Consensus is lagging.\n- Sentiment: mildly euphoric on retail/social channels (\"86% surge in 6 months\") but not extreme.\nCrowding risk score: 8/15 (short squeeze + retail + institutional overlap). Reduce cap by 1ppt \u2192 2\u20134%.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 6 \u2014 PORTFOLIO FIT\n\n- Correlation profile: beta 0.581 (vendor; low); high correlation with housing/consumer-cyclical factors.\n- Portfolio role: modest cyclical hedge within mostly-staples/quality book; net-cash balance sheet lowers\n  systemic tail risk.\n- Common bear triggers across portfolio: a 2008/2024-style housing recession would hit FLXS hard. Check\n  your other housing/consumer-discretionary positions \u2014 if you already own LZB/ETD/LEG, cap FLXS at 2%.\n- CVaR (worst-5% 12-mo loss): ~55\u201365% (cyclical revenuje -30%, multiple 8x, EPS halving). Stress-test:\n  at $41 scenario, portfolio impact = -50.6% position drawdown.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 6.5 \u2014 KELLY SIZING\n\nExpected return vs T0: (88.2\u201380.93)/80.93 = +9.0%; with ~30% annualized vol \u2192 Sharpe \u2248 0.30.\nFull Kelly \u2248 (expected excess return)/variance = (9.0% - 4.65%) / (30%)\u00b2 = 4.35% / 9% = 0.48% \u2192 effectively\n0\u20131% (because the risk-free at 4.65% is high). Half-Kelly = 0.24%. This reflects real math: a mid-teen\nrequired return leaves no edge at T0. **Position cap 2% initial; 3% after a 10% drawdown.**\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 7 \u2014 RISKS & CATALYSTS\n\nRisks:\n1. Tariffs (25% Section 232 on steel/foam/alloy inputs) \u2014 Ebit margin could compress to <7%.\n2. Housing recession \u2014 revenue -20%, EPS $4 or lower.\n3. Raw materials and freight spikes.\n4. Management guidance pause \u2192 uncertainty premium.\n5. Retailer concentration / bankruptcy risk in wholesale channel.\n6. Litigation and periodic activist/control risk [Unconfirmed].\n\nCatalysts:\n1. FY2027 guidance reinstated (likely Feb 2027) with positive margin trajectory.\n2. Housing turnover recovery / mortgage-rate decline.\n3. New strategic account wins (the \"9th consecutive quarter of sales growth\" driver).\n4. A new control proposal at $90+ \u2014 board rejected $20.80 in 2022, implying a much higher private\n   reservation price.\n5. Continued buyback (now only ~4.076M shares outstanding \u2014 greater sensitivity to buyback).\n\nPre-mortem \u2014 if FLXS fails in 5 years, why?\n- Tariffs erased US manufacturing cost-advantage, importers took 300bp of GM.\n- The board overpaid in a second self-tender during a housing bust.\n- E-commerce/fast-furniture structurally compressed the premium segment, and the \"durability\" message lost\n  to \"fashion/price.\"\nEarly warning indicators: gross margin <20%; two consecutive negative-revenue quarters; renewal of\nunsolicited bid below $60/share.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\u25a0 SECTION 7.5 \u2014 CORRELATION-ADJUSTED RISK\n\nTail correlation of FLXS with: growth/tech \u2014 0.2; housing/durables \u2014 0.7; financials \u2014 0.4. During 2022\ninflation-stagflation shock, cyclical manufacturers fell 35\u201345% despite profitable balance sheets. CVaR\nestimate above stands. Because the current portfolio is assumed to hold staples/quality, adding FLXS\nincreases portfolio CVaR modestly; if the portfolio already tilts cyclical, size to 1%.\n\n\